librt/spawn: use vfork() on NOMMU instead of returning ENOSYS

posix_spawn() uses vfork() when the requested attributes are "safe" and there
are no file actions, and fork() otherwise. On a NOMMU target there is no fork(),
so the else branch returns ENOSYS -- and the conditions that lead to it are the
ordinary ones, not exotic ones:

  - any file actions at all
  - POSIX_SPAWN_SETSIGDEF or SETSIGMASK, without POSIX_SPAWN_USEVFORK

glib always builds a posix_spawn_file_actions_t, even when it is empty, so its
posix_spawn fast path can never succeed on NOMMU; it falls back to the fork()
that does not exist. The interface most useful to a system without fork() is
thus unavailable on exactly that system.

Use vfork() unconditionally when there is no MMU. Everything __spawni() does
between the fork point and the execve is safe in a vfork child:

  - it never returns. Every path ends in execve() or _exit(), so the frame it
    shares with the suspended parent is never popped out from under it, which
    is the hazard vfork() is warned about.
  - sigprocmask(), sigaction(), sched_*(), setpgid() and seteuid() change
    per-task state. vfork() is CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK without CLONE_SIGHAND, so
    the child has its own handler table and the parent's dispositions are
    untouched.
  - the file actions are dup2(), open() and close(). vfork() does not pass
    CLONE_FILES either, so the child has its own descriptor table.
  - the only writes to shared memory are the alloca() used for the PATH search,
    below the parent's frame and released by the exec.

The visible difference from fork() is that the parent is suspended until the
child execs or exits, which for posix_spawn() is the intended flow anyway.

The MMU path is unchanged.

Upstream status: not submitted.

